    "result_text": "Build passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added per-country national-number-length validation to the E.164 telephone normalizer.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `TelephoneE164.normalizeOrNull()` (`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/TelephoneE164.java`): after the existing generic 8\u201315 total-digit check, I added a check on the national-number length keyed off the country code embedded in the normalized digits:\n- `+61` (Australia) \u2192 must have exactly **9** national digits (digits after `61`)\n- `+1` (NANP) \u2192 must have exactly **10** national digits (digits after `1`)\n\nA wrong length returns `null`, which `TelephoneE164.normalize()` turns into an `InvalidTelephoneException`, mapped by the existing `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler` to **400**. Unknown country codes still fall back to the generic 8\u201315 rule.\n\nI also updated the Javadoc on `TelephoneE164` and the message in `InvalidTelephoneException` to document the new rule accurately.\n\n**Verification:**\n- The new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength()` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes.\n- The valid path is preserved: `uniqueTelephone()` produces `04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 drops leading `0` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits \u2713.\n- Full test suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 0 failures.",
